<DocumentationLayout
  title="Getting Started with the API"
  description="Authenticate and make your first API request in under 5 minutes"
>
  <h1>Getting Started with the API</h1>

  <p>
    This guide walks you through obtaining an API key, making your first
    authenticated request, and understanding rate limits. By the end, you'll
    be ready to integrate the Pixelated Empathy API into your application.
  </p>

  <h2>Table of Contents</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><a href="#prerequisites">Prerequisites</a></li>
    <li><a href="#create-api-key">Create an API Key</a></li>
    <li><a href="#first-request">Make Your First Request</a></li>
    <li><a href="#authentication">Authentication Methods</a></li>
    <li><a href="#rate-limits">Rate Limits & Quotas</a></li>
    <li><a href="#error-handling">Error Handling</a></li>
    <li><a href="#next-steps">Next Steps</a></li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="prerequisites">Prerequisites</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A Pixelated Empathy account with developer access</li>
    <li>Node.js 18+ or Python 3.9+ installed (for SDK usage)</li>
    <li><code>curl</code> or any HTTP client for testing</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="create-api-key">Create an API Key</h2>
  <p>
    API keys are managed through the admin dashboard. Keys are scoped to
    specific permissions and have per-key rate limits.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Navigate to the <a href="/admin/api-keys">API Key Management</a> dashboard</li>
    <li>Click "Create New Key"</li>
    <li>Select the scopes your application needs:
      <ul>
        <li><code>read</code> — Read access to non-PHI endpoints</li>
        <li><code>write</code> — Create/update resources</li>
        <li><code>developer:manage</code> — Manage API keys (rotation, revocation)</li>
        <li><code>analytics:read</code> — Access analytics endpoints</li>
        <li><code>memory:read</code> / <code>memory:write</code> — Memory API access</li>
        <li><code>admin</code> — Full admin access (use with caution)</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>Set a name and optional expiration date</li>
    <li>Copy the generated key — it's shown only once</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout callout-warning">
    <strong>Security:</strong> Store your API key securely. Never commit it to
    version control or expose it in client-side code. Use environment variables
    or a secrets manager.
  </div>

  <h2 id="first-request">Make Your First Request</h2>
  <p>Once you have an API key, make a test request:</p>

  <pre><code class="language-bash">curl -X GET https://api.pixelatedempathy.com/api/v1/health \
  -H "X-API-Key: dev_your_api_key_here"</code></pre>

  <p>Expected response:</p>
  <pre><code class="language-json">{
  "status": "healthy",
  "version": "1",
  "timestamp": "2026-07-23T00:00:00Z"
}</code></pre>

  <p>The response includes the <code>X-API-Version</code> header indicating the API version that served your request.</p>

  <h2 id="authentication">Authentication Methods</h2>

  <h3>API Key (Recommended for Server-Side)</h3>
  <p>
    Include your API key in the <code>X-API-Key</code> header on every request.
    This is the simplest method for server-to-server integrations.
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-bash">curl -X GET https://api.pixelatedempathy.com/api/v1/search?q=anxiety \
  -H "X-API-Key: dev_your_api_key_here"</code></pre>

  <h3>JWT Bearer Token (Session-Based)</h3>
  <p>
    For applications using user login flows, use the JWT bearer token from
    the authentication endpoint. This is useful for browser-based applications.
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-bash"># First, authenticate to get a JWT
curl -X POST https://api.pixelatedempathy.com/api/auth/login \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"email": "user@example.com", "password": "..."}'

# Then use the token
curl -X GET https://api.pixelatedempathy.com/api/v1/profile \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIs..."</code></pre>

  <h3>Scopes</h3>
  <p>
    Each API key has assigned scopes. Requests requiring a scope your key
    doesn't have will return <code>403 Forbidden</code>. Check the
    <a href="/docs/api/swagger">API reference</a> for required scopes per
    endpoint.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rate-limits">Rate Limits & Quotas</h2>
  <p>
    Every API response includes rate limit headers:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>X-RateLimit-Limit</code> — Maximum requests per minute for your tier</li>
    <li><code>X-RateLimit-Remaining</code> — Requests remaining in the current window</li>
    <li><code>X-RateLimit-Reset</code> — Unix timestamp when the window resets</li>
    <li><code>X-RateLimit-Daily-Remaining</code> — Daily quota remaining</li>
    <li><code>X-RateLimit-Monthly-Remaining</code> — Monthly quota remaining</li>
  </ul>

  <p>When you exceed a limit, the API returns:</p>
  <pre><code class="language-json">HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests
Content-Type: application/json
Retry-After: 12

{
  "error": "rate_limit_exceeded",
  "message": "Rate limit exceeded. Retry after 12 seconds.",
  "remaining": 0,
  "resetTimeMs": 180000
}</code></pre>

  <h2 id="error-handling">Error Handling</h2>
  <p>The API uses standard HTTP status codes:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>200</code> — Success</li>
    <li><code>201</code> — Created</li>
    <li><code>400</code> — Bad request (validation error)</li>
    <li><code>401</code> — Unauthorized (missing or invalid key)</li>
    <li><code>403</code> — Forbidden (insufficient scopes)</li>
    <li><code>404</code> — Not found</li>
    <li><code>429</code> — Rate limit exceeded</li>
    <li><code>500</code> — Internal server error</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Error responses include a <code>message</code> field with details:</p>
  <pre><code class="language-json">{
  "error": "validation_error",
  "message": "Query parameter 'q' is required",
  "details": { "field": "q", "issue": "required" }
}</code></pre>

